WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:01.801
- From the very beginning,

00:00:01.801 --> 00:00:03.470
I got an email from someone

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
that knows the Cuomo
family very well

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:09.409
that said to me, you know,
"I'm sorry for your loss.",

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
something we never heard from
the Governor, by the way.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.213
"I'm sorry for your loss,

00:00:13.213 --> 00:00:16.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
your fight is a valiant
one, but watch your back."

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:19.452
(suspenseful music)

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.858 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
joining me today

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.627 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
is the senior
meteorologist at Fox News,

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.430 line:0%
author of the new book,
Make Your Own Sunshine:

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:33.333
Inspiring stories of
people who find a light

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:34.934
in dark times,

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:38.772 align:start size:97% position:3%
and a woman who is not a
big fan of New York Governor

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:42.042 align:start size:84% position:16%
Andrew Cuomo, Janice
Dean, finally.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.743
Welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:44.944
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
Thank you for having
me, my friend.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:48.615 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How'd you like that
little addition that I gave

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:52.352
to your bio there, did that,
does that feel right to you?

00:00:52.352 --> 00:00:55.321 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, I gotta tell you,
if you told me a year ago

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:58.391
that I would be in
the middle of a storm

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.226 align:start size:84% position:16%
involving the
Governor of New York

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.128 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would have said,
"What are you drinking?

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:04.964
"And where can I get some?"

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.167
- Well, you certainly are.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:08.735
And you know, my audience knows

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.804
I try to do these
interviews really

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:12.072
as free form as possible.

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.107 align:start size:81% position:19%
But I thought with
this interview,

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:16.209 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm gonna do it a little
more intentionally,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
because the book which
I've been reading

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:21.014
over the last couple
of days, it's so light,

00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:25.418
and warm and decent, and it
puts a smile on your face.

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
But this last year, you really
have stepped into a fight

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:30.490
that so many of us
are dealing with it

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.358
at so many different levels.

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:36.429
So for people that have no
idea about what has gone on

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.466 align:start size:97% position:3%
in your life in the past
year, can you just lay it out,

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:41.101
how did this all start,

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:44.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
and when did you
decide to really get
into the public fight?

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:48.007 line:0%
- March 25th was the order

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.643 line:0%
which Governor Cuomo
put his signature on

00:01:50.643 --> 00:01:52.612 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
that allowed
COVID-positive patients

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:55.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
into nursing homes,
9,000 of them.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:58.518
And that was a fairly
recent development.

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.554 align:start position:0%
The reason why I'm so interested
in this topic is because

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.023
we lost my husband's parents

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.959
in separate elder
care facilities,

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.461
in late March, we lost his dad,

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:12.432
and in April, about two weeks
later, we lost his mother,

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:15.201
and obviously tragic,

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:19.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
I never planned to be
someone who is a spokesperson

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:22.876
on behalf of no, my
husband's parents,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
and thousands of
others that we lost,

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:27.013
but I felt like I had to,

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.450 align:start position:0%
because when I saw the
Governor on television programs

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.319
like his brother's
on CNN joking around,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:37.524
and not even approaching
the nursing home issue,

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:40.193
that's when I decided to
get a little bit angry,

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:44.664 align:start position:0%
because I felt like my husband's
parents and the situation

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.700 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they were presented
with, unknowingly,

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.471 align:start position:0%
was not being represented
fairly in the mainstream media.

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.840
And, you know, going
back to his parents,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.143
we didn't even know there
was a March 25th order.

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.012
And the plan was,

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.481
we were supposed to
have both of them at

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:04.551
an assisted living residence
close to us on Long Island.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:07.520
I feel, you know,
kindred spirits

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.723 align:start size:81% position:19%
with people who
have to talk about

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:12.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
what to do with
their aging parents.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:14.427
Their health was failing them,

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:18.097
they lived in a four-story walk
up in Brooklyn for 50 years,

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:21.067 align:start position:0%
we had many conversations
about getting them out of there

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:23.803
because they were going to
have issues trying to go up

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.305
and down the stairs,

00:03:25.305 --> 00:03:27.307
so for many years, the
conversation was there,

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:29.375
we wanted to get them
into a different place,

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:32.212
we offered to help
them monetarily,

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:34.881
and they just never
wanted to take that.

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.449
They just said, "We're
just gonna stick it out,

00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:37.684
"we love it here,

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:40.253 align:start size:84% position:16%
"and we'll deal with
it as it comes."

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:42.922
And as it happened,
his dad got dementia,

00:03:42.922 --> 00:03:44.624
his health was failing him,

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:49.329
his mom was very spry for,
you know, a 75, 76 year old,

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:52.432
but she had back problems,
she had problems walking,

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:53.700
and so it got to the point

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.969 align:start size:84% position:16%
where we had aids
going to visit them,

00:03:55.969 --> 00:03:57.670
that wasn't working out,

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:02.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
so we took his mom to look
at assisted facilities,

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
and she loved the one
close to us on Long Island.

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:07.213
She signed the paperwork.

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:09.315
His father was in bad health,

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:10.850
so we had to get
him in better shape

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.418
before he could
he could join her

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:14.687
in the assisted
living residence,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.489
and that's when COVID happened.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:19.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we hadn't even
cleared out their apartment,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.662
we were still paying
the rent for them,

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:24.163
so it was very recent

00:04:24.163 --> 00:04:26.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we had put them in
the elder care facilities,

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:29.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
they had never been
separated in their lives,

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:32.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we were just hoping
to get Mickey, his father,

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:33.473
in better shape.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:36.909
So COVID happened, we were
in quarantine in early March,

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:41.014 align:start size:97% position:3%
we weren't able to see them,
we were getting updates

00:04:41.014 --> 00:04:43.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
from their separate
elder care facilities,

00:04:43.216 --> 00:04:46.519
Sean was able to talk
to his mom every day,

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:48.755
because she was, you
know, she was lucid,

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.924 align:start size:97% position:3%
his dad, obviously, it was
tough because he had dementia.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.927 align:start size:97% position:3%
We got a call on a Saturday
morning in late March,

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:57.063
not even knowing he was ill,

00:04:57.063 --> 00:05:00.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they said that Mickey
was not feeling well,

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:01.501
and he was running a fever.

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:04.170
And so of course
we were concerned,

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.039
and we were talking about
what we were gonna do,

00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:09.475
and three hours later, we get
a call saying he was dead.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:13.980
We had no idea he
had died of COVID

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.616
until we saw the
death certificate,

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:20.086
and Sean had to deliver
the news to his mother,

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:24.190
and to say that she
was heartbroken is
an understatement.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:28.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
She got sick in her
assisted living facility,

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:30.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
was diagnosed with COVID
when they brought her

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:33.166
to the hospital and she
died in the hospital,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.201
several days later.

00:05:35.201 --> 00:05:38.571 align:start size:97% position:3%
The last thing she asked my
husband to do was put her name

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.440 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the Easter
gifts for the kids.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.777 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I never wanted
to tell their story,

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
my husband is a
very private person.

00:05:46.913 --> 00:05:49.716
He's not on TV, he's
not a broadcaster.

00:05:49.716 --> 00:05:53.286
He's a firefighter, 25
years in the department.

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:55.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
His dad was a
firefighter, as well.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:58.424
But when I wasn't
seeing the coverage

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.293
of the nursing home issue,

00:06:00.293 --> 00:06:02.061
and the fact that
I was finding out

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.831 align:start position:0%
that Cuomo wasn't counting
those that died in the hospital

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.566
like my mother-in-law,

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.234 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's when I
decided it was time

00:06:08.234 --> 00:06:10.603
to tell my story, our story,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:14.507
so that at least somebody,
anybody would, you know,

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
find out that this
was actually something

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:18.144
that was happening in New York.

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:19.645
- Yeah, boy, all right.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.781
There's so many sort of off
shoots where we go there,

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:23.082
first off, I have to say, I
don't know if you know this,

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:24.884
but I'm actually from
Long Island as well,

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.652 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I know a lot of Long
Island firefighters,

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:29.088
so I have no doubt that
Sean is a great guy,

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.124 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause every single
one of them that I know

00:06:31.124 --> 00:06:33.259
are great people.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:36.295
Well, I guess first, just for
a little more on the timeline,

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.298
so how long was it that your
folks didn't see each other,

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
or your in-laws
didn't see each other

00:06:41.434 --> 00:06:44.237
before you got that call
about your father-in-law?

00:06:45.505 --> 00:06:47.140
- Well, you know,
it started out that

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:50.209
both Mickey and Dee were in
the nursing home together,

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:51.911
but his mother was
in better shape,

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:54.113 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that's when she went to
the assisted living residence,

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:57.683 align:start position:0%
and I would say they were
probably apart for a few weeks.

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:01.654
So they were apart
for a few weeks,

00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:04.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they knew that they would
be together at some point

00:07:04.457 --> 00:07:06.559
at the assisted
living residence.

00:07:07.493 --> 00:07:10.830
So, you know, it
wasn't very long

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
that COVID happened when
they were in this situation.

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:19.439
- Do you remember
when Cuomo signed

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
that piece of legislation
or whatever it was,

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:25.545 align:start position:0%
do you remember thinking,
uh-oh, this could be a problem?

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:27.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were so many
things happening at once,

00:07:27.079 --> 00:07:29.048 align:start size:88% position:13%
it was almost like
impossible to make sense

00:07:29.048 --> 00:07:30.483
of any of it back then.

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:32.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know it's hard to
remember just a year ago.

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:34.187
- We didn't know about it, Dave.

00:07:34.187 --> 00:07:37.990
I mean, honestly I found out
about it after they had died.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.225
No one told us about it.

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:41.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I remember
the red flag

00:07:41.194 --> 00:07:45.331
after the fact was that we
got a call about Mickey,

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:48.401 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would say about a week
before he passed away

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:51.003
saying that they were gonna
move him to another floor,

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.172
because they were going
to allow for more people

00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:54.774
to come into the residence,

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:57.176
but they didn't say
infected patients,

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.111
and we had no idea.

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:01.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
We only learned
after they had died

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.316
that there was a
mandate in place

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:05.485
to put COVID-positive patients.

00:08:05.485 --> 00:08:07.487
So, you know, that's a big deal.

00:08:07.487 --> 00:08:10.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think whose responsibility
would that have been,

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:12.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
the nursing home, the
assisted living residence?

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.693
Someone should have told us.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:15.228
But the other point of that

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:17.663
is that we weren't
able to see them.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.499
You know, we were in quarantine,

00:08:19.499 --> 00:08:23.503
Sean did see his mom
before she got sick.

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:26.239 align:start size:81% position:19%
He dropped off
flowers and a card

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:27.840
after his dad had passed away,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:32.311
and he was able to see her
six feet away with a mask on,

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:35.214
but he wasn't able to go
and hug her, you know,

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:37.750 align:start size:84% position:16%
so there were all
these things in place

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.952
where we physically
couldn't do anything,

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.421
you were almost jailed.

00:08:42.421 --> 00:08:45.291
You know, you couldn't move,
you couldn't find things out,

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:46.425
you couldn't go see them,

00:08:46.425 --> 00:08:48.227
you couldn't bring them to you.

00:08:48.227 --> 00:08:50.963
You know, obviously if I had
known about a March 25th order,

00:08:50.963 --> 00:08:52.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
we would have done
things very differently,

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.734
we would have found a
way to bring them here,

00:08:54.734 --> 00:08:56.869
you know, his sister-in-law
would have taken one of them,

00:08:56.869 --> 00:08:58.204
we would have done something.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.606
- Yeah, look, we all
know hindsight is 2020,

00:09:00.606 --> 00:09:02.174
but do you think in retrospect

00:09:02.174 --> 00:09:04.143
that the nursing
homes themselves

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:05.811
should have done
different things,

00:09:05.811 --> 00:09:07.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
should have just
protested the order?

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.248
I mean, do you think that they,

00:09:09.248 --> 00:09:10.883
some of them may
have sort of known,

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know that we
have more info now,

00:09:12.652 --> 00:09:15.688
so I know it's tough to do
that kind of quarterbacking.

00:09:17.823 --> 00:09:20.860
- From what I know, and the
people that I've talked with

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.763
that work in nursing homes,

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:26.566 align:start size:97% position:3%
they did not want to take
the COVID-positive patients.

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:29.001
They felt they had no choice,

00:09:29.001 --> 00:09:31.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
and if you read the order,
they weren't allowed

00:09:31.470 --> 00:09:32.972
to even test them.

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:36.776
They were prohibited from
testing them for COVID.

00:09:36.776 --> 00:09:41.414
So, you know, that
in itself is like

00:09:42.315 --> 00:09:43.616
if you couldn't test them,

00:09:43.616 --> 00:09:46.485
then you didn't know if
they even had the virus.

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:47.453
So I think --

00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:48.788
- What could be the logic

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:50.856 align:start size:88% position:13%
behind not being
allowed to test people,

00:09:50.856 --> 00:09:52.959 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you're bringing
into the home?

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:55.661 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, this is a
bigger, broader subject

00:09:55.661 --> 00:09:57.530 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I think we
need to investigate.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.432
There are several investigations

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:01.601 align:start size:97% position:3%
into the nursing home issue,
which I am grateful for.

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.337
There's a federal one,
and there's an FBI one.

00:10:04.337 --> 00:10:06.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
And what I wanna
know is first of all,

00:10:06.205 --> 00:10:08.374
the origins of the
March 25th order,

00:10:08.374 --> 00:10:09.609
where did that come from?

00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:11.644
Because it's not
based on science.

00:10:11.644 --> 00:10:14.180 align:start size:88% position:13%
We all knew that putting
infected patients

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:18.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
into where our most vulnerable
live is not scientific.

00:10:18.217 --> 00:10:20.086 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was going to
spread like wildfire,

00:10:20.086 --> 00:10:21.887 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is what our
Governor said, as well,

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:26.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
that it would be like
wildfire through dry grass.

00:10:26.258 --> 00:10:28.728
So I wanna know where
that order came from.

00:10:28.728 --> 00:10:30.329
There are reports out there

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:32.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the governor was
not listening to science,

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:34.567
he was listening to
hospital lobbyists,

00:10:34.567 --> 00:10:36.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
who were saying to him,
- Right.

00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:39.639
"We can't take these
patients for very long.

00:10:39.639 --> 00:10:41.874
"You know, they're not
maybe paying customers.

00:10:41.874 --> 00:10:44.410
"We need to get them
over to someplace else

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:47.046 line:0%
"so that we can allow for
more people to come in."

00:10:47.046 --> 00:10:49.782 line:0%
At the beginning of the
pandemic, he had no idea.

00:10:49.782 --> 00:10:53.019 line:0%
We thought it was gonna be
thousands upon thousands

00:10:53.019 --> 00:10:54.520 align:start size:81% position:19%
of people that
were going to come

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
into the hospitals and
take over the beds.

00:10:56.989 --> 00:11:00.092 align:start size:84% position:16%
So at the beginning,
I don't blame him

00:11:00.092 --> 00:11:02.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
for not really knowing
what to do and sort of say,

00:11:02.395 --> 00:11:04.530 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Well maybe we should
put them the back end.",

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:07.700
but it was in place for 46 days.

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:11.804
And then it was reversed and
scrubbed from the website.

00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:14.106
So that should also
tell you something.

00:11:14.106 --> 00:11:16.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think there is
corruption involved,

00:11:16.842 --> 00:11:18.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think there is a
criminal aspect to it,

00:11:18.744 --> 00:11:21.180
I think it involves
hospital lobbyists,

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.150
and the curious thing
as well is that Cuomo

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.886
put it in place for
a blanket immunity

00:11:26.886 --> 00:11:28.287
for all of the nursing homes,

00:11:28.287 --> 00:11:30.322
so that we could not sue them,

00:11:30.322 --> 00:11:33.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
just a couple of days
after that March 25th order

00:11:33.225 --> 00:11:34.927
was put into effect.

00:11:34.927 --> 00:11:36.162
- Have you talked to lawyers

00:11:36.162 --> 00:11:38.631
about how that could
possibly be legal?

00:11:38.631 --> 00:11:39.899 align:start size:88% position:13%
'Cause when I was doing
a little research,

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:43.002
that seems crazy that
the Governor in effect

00:11:43.002 --> 00:11:45.538
is allowed to say, "I'm
going to make a policy.

00:11:45.538 --> 00:11:47.473
"And regardless of the
results of the policy

00:11:47.473 --> 00:11:50.176
"I'm going to make
sure that the places

00:11:50.176 --> 00:11:52.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
"that I'm forcing to do
something cannot be sued."

00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:55.581
I mean, I don't even see how
that could be remotely legal.

00:11:55.581 --> 00:11:59.552
- Well, they're trying now
in Albany to reverse that,

00:11:59.552 --> 00:12:02.288
that executive order, so
that the nursing homes

00:12:02.288 --> 00:12:04.256
can be held accountable.

00:12:04.256 --> 00:12:05.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
Listen, it's not
about suing people --

00:12:05.624 --> 00:12:07.193
- Do you want them to
be held accountable?

00:12:07.193 --> 00:12:09.361
I mean, is that with, yeah.

00:12:09.361 --> 00:12:12.264
- I'll never get my
in-laws back, right?

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:15.134
And the nursing homes,

00:12:15.134 --> 00:12:18.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
I felt like they were
just doing what they could

00:12:18.437 --> 00:12:20.439 align:start size:81% position:19%
with the situation
that they had.

00:12:20.439 --> 00:12:22.842
They were wearing garbage bags.

00:12:22.842 --> 00:12:24.276
They had no PPE.

00:12:24.276 --> 00:12:26.378
That's another separate issue is

00:12:26.378 --> 00:12:28.714
they didn't have the wherewithal

00:12:28.714 --> 00:12:31.417 align:start size:97% position:3%
to deal with all of these
patients that were coming in,

00:12:31.417 --> 00:12:32.918
and you listen,

00:12:34.353 --> 00:12:37.423 align:start size:97% position:3%
they took good care of my
husband's parents, you know,

00:12:37.423 --> 00:12:41.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
before COVID, so, and we're
not getting any answers

00:12:41.894 --> 00:12:43.729
from them either, I
think they're afraid,

00:12:43.729 --> 00:12:46.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
I know that the nursing
home people, the executives

00:12:46.832 --> 00:12:50.069
and the folks that work
in the nursing homes,

00:12:50.069 --> 00:12:51.570
they are afraid to talk.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.773
They don't wanna
lose their jobs.

00:12:53.773 --> 00:12:56.809 align:start position:0%
And I do think that there needs
to be more whistleblowers.

00:12:56.809 --> 00:12:58.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's why I think
there needs to be

00:12:58.277 --> 00:13:00.346
a real investigation
with subpoena power,

00:13:00.346 --> 00:13:02.448 align:start size:84% position:16%
so people in the
nursing home industry

00:13:02.448 --> 00:13:03.816
and the hospital industry,

00:13:03.816 --> 00:13:05.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
those that really
wanna tell the truth,

00:13:05.651 --> 00:13:07.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
they can come forward
without thinking

00:13:07.887 --> 00:13:10.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they're gonna
lose their livelihoods.

00:13:10.189 --> 00:13:14.193
- So when did you decide to
really go public about this?

00:13:14.193 --> 00:13:16.295
'Cause as you said, your
husband's a private person,

00:13:16.295 --> 00:13:18.364 align:start size:88% position:13%
and although you're a
public person, you know,

00:13:18.364 --> 00:13:20.966 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's his parents,
they're your in-laws,

00:13:20.966 --> 00:13:23.269
it's not something that it
would be that comfortable

00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:25.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
talking about probably
'cause it's deeply personal,

00:13:25.471 --> 00:13:26.505
all that kinda stuff.

00:13:27.706 --> 00:13:30.209
- Well, when I started
to see the information

00:13:30.209 --> 00:13:32.278
about the March 25th order,

00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:35.114
and then I started to
see the information,

00:13:35.114 --> 00:13:38.684
the Daily Caller was the
one that put out a report

00:13:38.684 --> 00:13:40.486 align:start size:84% position:16%
saying that Cuomo
wasn't counting those

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:42.021
that died in the hospital.

00:13:42.021 --> 00:13:44.824
And I thought that that was
bizarre, because it's like,

00:13:44.824 --> 00:13:47.293 align:start size:84% position:16%
if there's a car
crash on the highway,

00:13:47.293 --> 00:13:49.929
and the person doesn't,
unfortunately, you know,

00:13:49.929 --> 00:13:51.897
tragically die on the highway,

00:13:51.897 --> 00:13:53.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they're transported
to the hospital,

00:13:53.766 --> 00:13:55.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
does that mean that's
a hospital death?

00:13:55.601 --> 00:13:57.102
No, of course not.

00:13:57.102 --> 00:14:00.039
It's still a car crash that
happened on the highway.

00:14:00.039 --> 00:14:03.042
So that to me was curious,

00:14:03.042 --> 00:14:05.110
and I started seeing
the governor going on

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:08.113 align:start size:97% position:3%
all of these television
programs and talking about,

00:14:08.113 --> 00:14:10.883
you know, how great
his leadership was,

00:14:10.883 --> 00:14:13.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
New York tough, you
know, bending that curve.

00:14:13.519 --> 00:14:14.920
He was selling a poster.

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:19.491 align:start position:0%
He was selling a poster
showing that bend of the curve,

00:14:19.491 --> 00:14:23.062
which was like a mountain,
and had all the COVID cases.

00:14:23.062 --> 00:14:24.897
And I thought to
myself, how gross

00:14:24.897 --> 00:14:28.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
that he's using COVID
cases, my husband's parents

00:14:28.934 --> 00:14:32.137
are on that curve that
he supposedly crushed.

00:14:32.137 --> 00:14:33.405
And then on the poster,

00:14:33.405 --> 00:14:34.640
there was all these
like little pictures

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:36.642
of the things that he
loved about himself,

00:14:36.642 --> 00:14:40.446
like it's his dog
and a giant q-tip,

00:14:40.446 --> 00:14:43.048
because of the, you know, the
show that he was putting on

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:46.518
with his brother, Chris
Cuomo on television.

00:14:46.518 --> 00:14:48.420 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was the breaking
point for me.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.223 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was thinking about
writing an op-ed

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:54.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
about the situation
because it was,

00:14:54.026 --> 00:14:56.629
I thought I wanted to
tell my family story

00:14:56.629 --> 00:14:58.130
to bring awareness.

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:00.833
But when I saw Cuomo on with
his brother joking around,

00:15:00.833 --> 00:15:05.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
like, who's the favorite
son and love gov,

00:15:05.137 --> 00:15:09.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the big giant
q-tip, I lost my mind.

00:15:09.308 --> 00:15:11.176
I just thought to myself,

00:15:11.176 --> 00:15:14.079 align:start size:88% position:13%
this is, what kind of
universe am I living in,

00:15:14.079 --> 00:15:17.016
with thousands upon
thousands of dead,

00:15:17.016 --> 00:15:20.619
the body bags are piling up
outside of nursing homes.

00:15:20.619 --> 00:15:22.821 align:start size:88% position:13%
We weren't able to
have wakes or funerals.

00:15:22.821 --> 00:15:27.259 align:start position:0%
We were, we luckily we knew
someone who owned a funeral home

00:15:27.259 --> 00:15:29.561 align:start size:84% position:16%
who went to school
with my sister-in-law,

00:15:29.561 --> 00:15:32.298
that helped us bury them,

00:15:32.298 --> 00:15:36.468 align:start size:88% position:13%
so that's when I decided
to talk to my friend,

00:15:36.468 --> 00:15:39.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
Tucker Carlson, I was
telling him what was going on

00:15:39.004 --> 00:15:41.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the nursing
homes and my concerns

00:15:41.173 --> 00:15:43.342 align:start size:84% position:16%
with what was
happening in New York.

00:15:43.342 --> 00:15:44.743
And he told me, he said,

00:15:44.743 --> 00:15:46.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
"You have an invitation
to come on my show,

00:15:46.679 --> 00:15:48.814
"if you ever wanna
tell the story."

00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:51.850 align:start size:81% position:19%
And so it was that
day after I saw

00:15:51.850 --> 00:15:55.421 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Cuomo Brothers comedy hour
that I went on Tucker Show.

00:15:55.421 --> 00:15:56.889
- That, I mean, okay.

00:15:56.889 --> 00:15:59.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
So everyone remembers
that interview with Chris

00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:03.228 align:start position:0%
and Governor Cuomo and you
know, the laughing and all that.

00:16:03.228 --> 00:16:05.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm gonna jump forward
for just a second.

00:16:05.064 --> 00:16:06.732
But when you saw Chris

00:16:06.732 --> 00:16:08.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause I'm sure you
saw it a few weeks ago

00:16:08.267 --> 00:16:11.637
say that he can no longer talk
about his brother on the show

00:16:11.637 --> 00:16:13.072
because it's personal.

00:16:13.072 --> 00:16:16.842 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, that's like basically
vomit-inducing stuff.

00:16:16.842 --> 00:16:19.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like we can give him the worst
sort of fluff interviews,

00:16:19.812 --> 00:16:22.081
we can both sit there
with our fake tans,

00:16:22.081 --> 00:16:25.184 align:start position:0%
and laugh hysterically and have
everyone write fluff pieces

00:16:25.184 --> 00:16:27.586 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the sexy Cuomo
brothers or whatever it is

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:29.188
that they're doing on HuffPo.

00:16:29.188 --> 00:16:32.725
And then, and now, oh, now
he's in a little hot water.

00:16:32.725 --> 00:16:36.795
Wouldn't be journalistic
if I talked about him now.

00:16:36.795 --> 00:16:38.030
- Right.

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:40.332
Can you imagine if that
happened on Fox News?

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:41.800
- Yeah, exactly.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.336
- Right, so I don't even
think Chris Cuomo should be

00:16:44.336 --> 00:16:45.738
on television right now.

00:16:46.638 --> 00:16:47.873
It's not ethical.

00:16:47.873 --> 00:16:50.409
I mean, Cuomo was a
huge story, right?

00:16:50.409 --> 00:16:52.244 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, the guy
could be impeached.

00:16:52.244 --> 00:16:55.881
There are, you know
motions going forward

00:16:55.881 --> 00:16:57.383
that he could be impeached.

00:16:57.383 --> 00:17:00.419
We have Me Too involved
now with eight women,

00:17:01.587 --> 00:17:04.423
he doesn't even do
like a news segment,

00:17:04.423 --> 00:17:07.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, he just
continues to do his show,

00:17:07.092 --> 00:17:09.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
ignoring the huge
elephant in the room.

00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:11.830
So yeah, it's quite
rich for him to say

00:17:11.830 --> 00:17:14.133
that he can no longer
interview his brother.

00:17:14.133 --> 00:17:16.235 align:start size:75% position:25%
It blows me.
- Yeah, so then,

00:17:16.235 --> 00:17:18.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
then the story blows up
and now you now you sort of

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:20.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
became the center of this
thing for a couple of weeks.

00:17:20.539 --> 00:17:21.974
It was like, oh, the
nursing home thing,

00:17:21.974 --> 00:17:23.342
people couldn't believe it,

00:17:23.342 --> 00:17:25.210
you're right in
the center of it.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:26.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
Since I spent so much
time in this show

00:17:26.645 --> 00:17:28.213
talking about cancel
culture and dealing

00:17:28.213 --> 00:17:29.481
with the mob and
everything else,

00:17:29.481 --> 00:17:31.216
can you just talk about,
a little bit about like

00:17:31.216 --> 00:17:33.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
the love and I'm
guessing some of the hate

00:17:33.118 --> 00:17:36.221
that you've received in
the midst of all this?

00:17:36.221 --> 00:17:39.458
- From the very beginning,
I got an email from someone

00:17:39.458 --> 00:17:42.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
that knows the Cuomo
family very well

00:17:42.461 --> 00:17:45.397
that said to me, you know,
"I'm sorry for your loss.",

00:17:45.397 --> 00:17:47.699 align:start size:97% position:3%
something we never heard from
the Governor, by the way,

00:17:47.699 --> 00:17:49.201
"I'm sorry for your loss.

00:17:49.201 --> 00:17:52.271
"Your fight is a valiant
one, but watch your back."

00:17:52.271 --> 00:17:53.739
- Right, yeah.

00:17:53.739 --> 00:17:57.342 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I knew that early on that
I was in for the fight,

00:17:57.342 --> 00:17:59.411
and I had heard that
he was vindictive,

00:17:59.411 --> 00:18:01.747
I also heard that
he was a bully,

00:18:03.115 --> 00:18:05.084 align:start position:0%
you know, there were many times
that I would do interviews

00:18:05.084 --> 00:18:07.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they would always
ask the Governor's office

00:18:07.052 --> 00:18:08.554
to make a comment,

00:18:08.554 --> 00:18:12.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
and twice the Governor's
office said, "Well, you know,

00:18:12.825 --> 00:18:14.526
"can you ask Jan
--" first of all,

00:18:14.526 --> 00:18:17.629 align:start size:97% position:3%
they never said we're
really sorry for your loss.

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:19.131
It was always,

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:23.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Can you ask Janice the
exact day her in-laws died

00:18:23.669 --> 00:18:25.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
"and what nursing
homes they were at?"

00:18:25.904 --> 00:18:29.942 align:start size:88% position:13%
So twice the Governor's
office asked that.

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:31.910
And then there was
one time there was

00:18:31.910 --> 00:18:33.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
a Daily Mail article
that was put out

00:18:33.979 --> 00:18:38.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they asked for a
comment and the quote was

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.786
"She's not a reliable
source on anything

00:18:40.786 --> 00:18:42.154
"except the weather."

00:18:42.154 --> 00:18:45.324 align:start size:88% position:13%
So, you know, and I
just thought to myself,

00:18:45.324 --> 00:18:48.127
well you know, that's not
very creative (laughs),

00:18:50.195 --> 00:18:54.299
but certainly on social media
in the early days of this,

00:18:54.299 --> 00:18:58.303
I was, you know, I was
definitely attacked.

00:18:58.303 --> 00:19:00.239
He was the most powerful guy,

00:19:00.239 --> 00:19:03.342 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, they were talking
about him being the President.

00:19:03.342 --> 00:19:06.345
He was, you know, the fact
that he was writing a book

00:19:06.345 --> 00:19:07.579
in the middle of the pandemic,

00:19:07.579 --> 00:19:09.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then again,
being on television,

00:19:09.481 --> 00:19:12.417 align:start size:84% position:16%
celebrating himself
winning an Emmy Award.

00:19:12.417 --> 00:19:14.219
And he had all these celebrities

00:19:14.219 --> 00:19:17.990 align:start size:97% position:3%
that were appearing with him
at his press conferences.

00:19:19.358 --> 00:19:21.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
I actually got into a
little bit of a Twitter spat

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:22.895
with Ben Stiller,

00:19:22.895 --> 00:19:25.864
because he was doing
a birthday celebration

00:19:25.864 --> 00:19:27.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
with Whoopi
Goldberg, by the way,

00:19:27.399 --> 00:19:28.901
they raised money for Cuomo --

00:19:28.901 --> 00:19:32.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How much was it per plate,
it for a virtual event.

00:19:32.004 --> 00:19:34.206
It was like 20 grand a
plate for a virtual event

00:19:34.206 --> 00:19:36.008
in the midst of all
of this, something --

00:19:36.008 --> 00:19:39.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, it was at least
$1,000 for the bare minimum.

00:19:41.213 --> 00:19:44.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I just thought, listen,
I'm gonna let Ben Stiller know

00:19:44.483 --> 00:19:46.552 align:start size:94% position:6%
what happened to the
nursing homes in New York.

00:19:46.552 --> 00:19:48.720
He's a New Yorker.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:51.089
And he came back at
me pretty quickly

00:19:51.089 --> 00:19:53.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
and was just like, "Well,
I like the Governor.

00:19:53.325 --> 00:19:55.060
"It's too bad that
you don't like him."

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:58.430
And you know, it was
just so dismissive,

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:03.435 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it didn't take long
for Ben Stiller to realize,

00:20:04.536 --> 00:20:06.104 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
you know, what my
family had been through,

00:20:06.104 --> 00:20:08.073 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and to his credit --
- Oh good.

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:10.209 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
which is more than
most people do,

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:12.778
he apologized and he
deleted his tweet.

00:20:12.778 --> 00:20:15.881
And I actually said,
I'm okay with that.

00:20:15.881 --> 00:20:18.217
You know, he didn't
know my family story.

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:19.384
Now he does.

00:20:19.384 --> 00:20:22.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe he's more educated
on the situation.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.258
And that's my whole thing is
if one person can be educated

00:20:26.258 --> 00:20:30.128
by me telling my story about
what's happening in New York,

00:20:30.128 --> 00:20:32.664
then all of that is worthwhile.

00:20:32.664 --> 00:20:33.932
- So what do you make of

00:20:33.932 --> 00:20:37.035
sort of where Cuomo
is at right now?

00:20:37.035 --> 00:20:40.339
Because you know, now
he's got, as you said,

00:20:40.339 --> 00:20:42.474 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's at least eight
women accusing him

00:20:42.474 --> 00:20:45.611
of some level of
sexual impropriety,

00:20:45.611 --> 00:20:47.179
I mean, I've said this
a million times before,

00:20:47.179 --> 00:20:49.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's like, okay, an
accusation is one thing,

00:20:49.047 --> 00:20:50.849
but of course you need proof,

00:20:50.849 --> 00:20:52.417
you need corroboration,

00:20:52.417 --> 00:20:56.521
but that's not the rules that
his team have been playing by

00:20:56.521 --> 00:20:57.856
for quite some time.

00:20:57.856 --> 00:20:59.758
Those weren't the rules
with Brett Kavanaugh

00:20:59.758 --> 00:21:02.461
or a litany of other
people on the right.

00:21:02.461 --> 00:21:05.864
So this is sort of his making.

00:21:05.864 --> 00:21:08.500
Just where do you sense he's
at right now between that,

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:10.435
and of course, all of this?

00:21:10.435 --> 00:21:12.004
- Well, he's not going to leave.

00:21:12.004 --> 00:21:13.372
He's already said that.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.809
And there is, you know, I
think it's kind of a sham.

00:21:17.809 --> 00:21:20.545
They say that they've
started the proceedings

00:21:20.545 --> 00:21:22.881
for impeachment in New
York, but it's going to

00:21:22.881 --> 00:21:26.084
take not weeks but months and
then they're done in June.

00:21:26.084 --> 00:21:28.720 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I don't think
that's going to happen.

00:21:29.554 --> 00:21:31.690
I am relying on the law.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:33.925
You know, the investigations
that are going on right now

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:36.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Federal investigation,
the FBI investigation,

00:21:36.361 --> 00:21:39.097
there was also a
DOJ investigation,

00:21:40.532 --> 00:21:44.369
you know, under bar that I
hope continues under Biden.

00:21:45.837 --> 00:21:48.373
The Me Too stuff, at first,

00:21:48.373 --> 00:21:50.375 align:start size:81% position:19%
I was a little
bit shocked by it,

00:21:50.375 --> 00:21:53.378 align:start position:0%
but now that I think of the
person that we're talking about

00:21:53.378 --> 00:21:55.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, sexual harassment
is not about sex.

00:21:55.847 --> 00:21:57.182
It's about power.

00:21:57.182 --> 00:21:59.985
And so his whole MO is about

00:21:59.985 --> 00:22:02.788 align:start size:97% position:3%
"I'm the most powerful guy
and I can do whatever I want."

00:22:02.788 --> 00:22:05.857
So now it doesn't surprise me
that he treated women badly

00:22:05.857 --> 00:22:08.193
and he's treated
other people badly.

00:22:08.193 --> 00:22:10.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, his
coworkers, Ron Kim,

00:22:10.796 --> 00:22:13.665 align:start size:88% position:13%
who is a friend of
mine, he is a Democrat.

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:16.768
He had an uncle that
died in a nursing home.

00:22:16.768 --> 00:22:20.138
He's been very vocal about
the nursing home situation.

00:22:20.138 --> 00:22:23.408 align:start size:97% position:3%
I am grateful for him because
he is raising awareness

00:22:23.408 --> 00:22:26.011 align:start size:88% position:13%
on his side of the
aisle of the situation.

00:22:26.011 --> 00:22:29.915
And he also came out
with his experience

00:22:29.915 --> 00:22:32.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the Governor,
calling him on a weekend,

00:22:32.451 --> 00:22:34.953
yelling at him and
saying, you know,

00:22:34.953 --> 00:22:39.958 align:start position:0%
"I'm gonna ruin you essentially,
if you go against me."

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:45.030
So I just, I hope
and pray, Dave,

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.666
that the law helps us out here,

00:22:47.666 --> 00:22:51.436
and that they get to the
bottom and he, you know,

00:22:51.436 --> 00:22:53.772
the truth, the whole truth
and nothing but the truth,

00:22:53.772 --> 00:22:55.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've always said, the only
thing I want right now

00:22:55.974 --> 00:22:59.077 align:start size:97% position:3%
is a bipartisan investigation
with subpoena power

00:22:59.077 --> 00:23:02.681
that puts the Governor
on the stand so to speak,

00:23:02.681 --> 00:23:05.016 align:start size:88% position:13%
his health commissioner,
Howard Zucker,

00:23:05.016 --> 00:23:08.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the people that surrounded
him that helped cover

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:09.554
up the numbers.

00:23:09.554 --> 00:23:11.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, that's another
big story is the fact

00:23:11.957 --> 00:23:16.061 align:start position:0%
that it was the AG, the
democratic AG here in New York,

00:23:16.061 --> 00:23:18.196
put out her own report saying

00:23:18.196 --> 00:23:22.801
that they purposely
under-counted the
nursing home deaths.

00:23:22.801 --> 00:23:25.937 align:start size:97% position:3%
Those that died in the
hospital were never counted.

00:23:25.937 --> 00:23:27.472
- I mean, listen,
as a New Yorker,

00:23:27.472 --> 00:23:30.509
I know how out of whack the
whole state government is

00:23:30.509 --> 00:23:32.677
in New York and half the people
that were in it 10 years ago

00:23:32.677 --> 00:23:33.845
are in jail right now.

00:23:33.845 --> 00:23:35.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's all very
bizarre in New York.

00:23:35.614 --> 00:23:38.250
Has this changed any of
your political beliefs

00:23:38.250 --> 00:23:39.751
as you've been in this fight?

00:23:41.253 --> 00:23:44.956
- You know, I've always said
I'm not a political person,

00:23:44.956 --> 00:23:47.559 align:start position:0%
I never have been, I'm
originally from Canada (laughs).

00:23:47.559 --> 00:23:49.394
You know, I was
born in Canada --

00:23:49.394 --> 00:23:50.929 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I think there's a
government up there, right?

00:23:50.929 --> 00:23:52.964
You got a government, that
guy with the fancy socks.

00:23:52.964 --> 00:23:55.033
- Yes, the fancy socks guy,

00:23:56.601 --> 00:23:59.538
you know my Mom is more
political than I am.

00:23:59.538 --> 00:24:01.106
You should meet her sometime.

00:24:01.106 --> 00:24:03.375
But you know, I'm the
meteorologist on Fox.

00:24:03.375 --> 00:24:05.811
I've always said the only
red and blue that I see

00:24:05.811 --> 00:24:09.548
on the map are areas of low
pressure and high pressure.

00:24:09.548 --> 00:24:12.150
And I stick with that.

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:15.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
I like the fact that nobody
knows who I voted for,

00:24:15.187 --> 00:24:16.688
when I got the job at Fox,

00:24:16.688 --> 00:24:19.791
it was never a prerequisite
to tell them who I voted for.

00:24:19.791 --> 00:24:23.228
So I will remain that way.

00:24:23.228 --> 00:24:27.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I will tell you,
I've often said to people

00:24:27.065 --> 00:24:31.303
"What if I worked for
a different channel

00:24:31.303 --> 00:24:35.540
"and the Governor that I was
going after was a Republican.

00:24:36.842 --> 00:24:39.177
"What do you think would happen

00:24:39.177 --> 00:24:42.047
"and how much coverage do you
think my story would get?"

00:24:44.483 --> 00:24:46.818
- Yeah, I get what
you're dropping.

00:24:46.818 --> 00:24:48.253
I get what you're dropping.

00:24:49.421 --> 00:24:51.490
All right, well, before
we move on from this,

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I do wanna talk
about the book, of course,

00:24:53.225 --> 00:24:54.693
I mean, is there anything else?

00:24:54.693 --> 00:24:56.561 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, actually wait, I do
have one other thing for sure,

00:24:56.561 --> 00:24:59.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is that we actually
delayed this interview taping

00:24:59.498 --> 00:25:03.535
a few days because you just
got the second COVID shot,

00:25:03.535 --> 00:25:06.571
and it sounds like that thing
really knocked you out, huh?

00:25:06.571 --> 00:25:07.806
- Oh, listen.

00:25:07.806 --> 00:25:10.942
I am pro-vaccine I have MS,

00:25:12.344 --> 00:25:15.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was diagnosed with multiple
sclerosis 16 years ago,

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:17.048
so I'm a compromised person.

00:25:17.983 --> 00:25:20.886
And so I believe
in vaccinations,

00:25:20.886 --> 00:25:24.189
and I was always
wanting to get one.

00:25:24.189 --> 00:25:28.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so my neurologist wrote
me a note and I was, you know,

00:25:28.159 --> 00:25:30.929
I was in line with the
other comorbidities.

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.666
The first shot was fine.

00:25:34.666 --> 00:25:36.668
You know, I had the
pain in the arm,

00:25:36.668 --> 00:25:38.169
and then the second shot,

00:25:38.169 --> 00:25:41.606
my husband also had had
the vaccinations as well.

00:25:41.606 --> 00:25:43.275
And he didn't do too badly.

00:25:43.275 --> 00:25:47.979
You know, for maybe 12 to
18 hours, he was flu-ish,

00:25:47.979 --> 00:25:50.282
and, you know, he had
the aches and pains,

00:25:50.282 --> 00:25:54.052
but man, I, you know, for,
I would say for 48 hours,

00:25:54.052 --> 00:25:58.657 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was like I had COVID, and
I had the worst headache.

00:25:58.657 --> 00:26:02.427
The headache was so bad that
I was nauseous the whole time.

00:26:02.427 --> 00:26:05.597 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you know, I couldn't
see my children,

00:26:05.597 --> 00:26:07.065
I couldn't look at anything,

00:26:07.065 --> 00:26:08.166
I couldn't see the TV,

00:26:08.166 --> 00:26:10.669
I was literally in a dark room.

00:26:10.669 --> 00:26:12.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, you know,
listen, I'm grateful.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've come out of it on the
other side, doing well.

00:26:15.774 --> 00:26:18.777 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I encourage everyone
to get the vaccine,

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:20.612
I believe in vaccinations,

00:26:20.612 --> 00:26:24.549
and I'm grateful for
the scientists that
came up with them,

00:26:24.549 --> 00:26:26.217
but yes, I will warn those,

00:26:26.217 --> 00:26:28.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
the second vaccination
can be a doozy.

00:26:28.687 --> 00:26:30.355 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Well, that is
the perfect segue,

00:26:30.355 --> 00:26:33.858
because you're saying all of
that, while you're smiling.

00:26:33.858 --> 00:26:36.261 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that really is,
that really is the point

00:26:36.261 --> 00:26:38.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the book and excellent
product placement behind you,

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.898
by the way --

00:26:39.898 --> 00:26:42.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
- (laughs) Thank you.
- Really, really spot on.

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:43.735 align:start size:94% position:6%
- But one of the things --
- You too.

00:26:43.735 --> 00:26:46.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh well, you know,
I've got people,

00:26:46.037 --> 00:26:47.939
but one of the things that I
really loved about the book is

00:26:47.939 --> 00:26:50.508
when I do my show every day,

00:26:50.508 --> 00:26:54.079
I try to do it with a little
bit of a smile on my face.

00:26:54.079 --> 00:26:56.715 align:start size:97% position:3%
I try to do it so that
people don't feel depressed

00:26:56.715 --> 00:26:58.016
after about the news.

00:26:58.016 --> 00:27:00.151 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not trying to just
bludge in everybody

00:27:00.151 --> 00:27:01.586
so that they're stuck watching,

00:27:01.586 --> 00:27:03.555
which is pretty much what CNN

00:27:03.555 --> 00:27:05.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
and a lot of the other
places are doing.

00:27:05.357 --> 00:27:08.360
So I guess first, how
did you kind of come

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:12.430
to that place where telling
people some of the good stuff

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:13.698
actually mattered to you?

00:27:15.133 --> 00:27:18.103
- I've been telling good news
stories for many years now.

00:27:18.103 --> 00:27:21.473
I do a feature on Fox News
Radio called the Dean's List.

00:27:22.574 --> 00:27:25.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's a 60-second,
feel-good story

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:28.947
about a person doing kind
things for someone else,

00:27:28.947 --> 00:27:30.949
or, you know doing
something that brings

00:27:30.949 --> 00:27:32.784
sunshine into someone's day.

00:27:32.784 --> 00:27:34.619
So I've been doing that
for a very long time.

00:27:34.619 --> 00:27:35.887
And I always thought to myself,

00:27:35.887 --> 00:27:38.023 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would love to make
this into a book

00:27:38.023 --> 00:27:40.692 align:start size:88% position:13%
where I could dive into
these stories each,

00:27:40.692 --> 00:27:42.560
make them a chapter.

00:27:42.560 --> 00:27:45.563
So this was that book.

00:27:45.563 --> 00:27:48.500
I started working on
it before the pandemic,

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:49.834
but I wrote most of it,

00:27:49.834 --> 00:27:53.171 align:start position:0%
and did a lot of the
interviews during the pandemic.

00:27:53.171 --> 00:27:55.206
And more than ever,

00:27:55.206 --> 00:27:57.575
even though we were socially
distancing from each other,

00:27:57.575 --> 00:28:00.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was finding these
stories all over the place

00:28:00.345 --> 00:28:03.548
of people doing wonderful
things for other people,

00:28:03.548 --> 00:28:05.617
we were adapting
as human beings,

00:28:05.617 --> 00:28:10.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, you saw people doing
car parades for birthdays.

00:28:10.288 --> 00:28:11.790
I got one of those.

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:14.025
My husband did that for
me, for my 50th birthday.

00:28:15.226 --> 00:28:18.063
So, and the other part
of this was, you know,

00:28:18.063 --> 00:28:19.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
I wanted to put this
book out in the universe

00:28:19.898 --> 00:28:21.399
for other people to read,

00:28:21.399 --> 00:28:24.703
to show them that there are
good people doing kind things

00:28:24.703 --> 00:28:26.538
for others all over the place.

00:28:26.538 --> 00:28:28.873
It's what unites us.

00:28:28.873 --> 00:28:31.943 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I really think that
kindness begets kindness,

00:28:31.943 --> 00:28:34.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
and those that do good
things for others,

00:28:34.312 --> 00:28:36.514
it gets passed along, right?

00:28:36.514 --> 00:28:39.417
So, but what I didn't
realize Dave is that

00:28:39.417 --> 00:28:42.053
I was the one that was
going to get the most

00:28:42.053 --> 00:28:44.022 align:start size:88% position:13%
out of writing this book
because I was talking

00:28:44.022 --> 00:28:46.825
to these wonderful people
during the darkest time

00:28:46.825 --> 00:28:48.326
for our family,

00:28:48.326 --> 00:28:50.361
and getting light and goodness

00:28:50.361 --> 00:28:52.864
from all of these stories and
interviews that I was doing,

00:28:52.864 --> 00:28:54.065
and every day, you know,

00:28:54.065 --> 00:28:56.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'd take my oldest son,
Matthew for a walk.

00:28:56.701 --> 00:28:58.970
And that would be our
topic of conversation.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:00.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
He'd be like, "Mom
who'd you talk to today,

00:29:00.905 --> 00:29:02.407
"tell me the story."

00:29:02.407 --> 00:29:04.342
And I would tell him the
story of the napkin notes,

00:29:04.342 --> 00:29:07.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
Dad who wrote napkin notes
for his daughter, Emma,

00:29:07.245 --> 00:29:09.047
when she was just a little girl,

00:29:09.047 --> 00:29:10.915
and how he continued doing that,

00:29:10.915 --> 00:29:12.751 align:start size:84% position:16%
even though he was
diagnosed with cancer,

00:29:12.751 --> 00:29:15.553
and thought he might not
live to see her graduate.

00:29:15.553 --> 00:29:18.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
So he wanted to make sure
he wrote napkin notes

00:29:18.089 --> 00:29:21.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
for hundreds of school
days in case they lost him.

00:29:21.259 --> 00:29:22.494
He's still here,

00:29:22.494 --> 00:29:24.496
and I talked to him
and I talked to Emma,

00:29:24.496 --> 00:29:27.198 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so those are the people
that brightened my day,

00:29:27.198 --> 00:29:30.769 align:start size:88% position:13%
when we were going
through such dark times.

00:29:30.769 --> 00:29:33.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
So that's the ironic
part of this is

00:29:33.772 --> 00:29:35.507
I got so much out of
writing this book,

00:29:35.507 --> 00:29:37.709
more so than I ever
thought I would.

00:29:37.709 --> 00:29:39.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so I wanna dive into
a couple of the stories,

00:29:39.644 --> 00:29:41.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause there's some really
great stuff in there,

00:29:41.246 --> 00:29:43.148
but I'm curious when you
were pitching the book,

00:29:43.148 --> 00:29:45.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
and bringing it to your
publishers and everything,

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:48.386
is selling a book
that's so positive,

00:29:48.386 --> 00:29:49.788
is that tough for those guys?

00:29:49.788 --> 00:29:51.523
'Cause if you just
look at a bookstore

00:29:51.523 --> 00:29:54.793
you know, front area,
what's in the window,

00:29:54.793 --> 00:29:58.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it's all basically
depressing stuff.

00:29:58.129 --> 00:29:59.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, the world's
coming to end,

00:29:59.664 --> 00:30:01.099
the pandemic's gonna kill you,

00:30:01.099 --> 00:30:02.333
Trump tried to kill you,

00:30:02.333 --> 00:30:03.868
and somebody else's
coming to get ya,

00:30:03.868 --> 00:30:05.937 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, blah, blah,
blah, blah, blah.

00:30:05.937 --> 00:30:08.339
- It's a tough sell,
I'm not gonna lie.

00:30:08.339 --> 00:30:11.109
And what I didn't tell you is,

00:30:11.109 --> 00:30:12.744
before the Dean's List happened,

00:30:12.744 --> 00:30:15.079
I did a good news
story on Fox News,

00:30:15.079 --> 00:30:18.516
a show called Happening Now
with my friend Jane Skinner.

00:30:18.516 --> 00:30:20.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
And she was the one that
came up with the Dean's List,

00:30:20.418 --> 00:30:22.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
because she thought, you
know, the news is so terrible,

00:30:22.420 --> 00:30:24.022
we need something
bright and cheery,

00:30:24.022 --> 00:30:27.592
and you do the weather and
you know, the weather is

00:30:27.592 --> 00:30:29.027 align:start size:81% position:19%
a lot of times
it's mostly sunny,

00:30:29.027 --> 00:30:30.695
but sometimes it's stormy,

00:30:30.695 --> 00:30:32.730
but why don't we work
on a good news story

00:30:32.730 --> 00:30:33.932
that we can use as a kicker,

00:30:33.932 --> 00:30:35.867
we call it a kicker
in the business.

00:30:35.867 --> 00:30:38.970 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
And so we started doing
that on television,

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:41.506 line:0%
but the news kind
of like took over

00:30:41.506 --> 00:30:45.543 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
and the Dean's List got
killed more often than not,

00:30:45.543 --> 00:30:47.979
but I was, you know, she was
always a cheerleader of it.

00:30:47.979 --> 00:30:50.181 align:start size:88% position:13%
So the moral of the
story is, you know what?

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:52.684
Sometimes the good
news doesn't sell,

00:30:54.118 --> 00:30:57.889
but I think now more than ever,
we need these stories, Dave,

00:30:57.889 --> 00:31:00.225 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, I really think
that we need to hear

00:31:00.225 --> 00:31:03.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
these hopeful good
stories about trying to be

00:31:03.261 --> 00:31:04.963
a better human being.

00:31:04.963 --> 00:31:06.464
And so that's my message.

00:31:06.464 --> 00:31:08.766
And I will tell you (laughs)

00:31:08.766 --> 00:31:11.035
that I got a lot of
people saying to me,

00:31:11.035 --> 00:31:12.904
"Okay, your book is coming out.

00:31:12.904 --> 00:31:15.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
"When are you gonna stop
with the Cuomo stuff?

00:31:15.006 --> 00:31:18.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Because we need the bright
(microphone cuts out)"

00:31:18.042 --> 00:31:19.244
so it'd be like,

00:31:19.244 --> 00:31:21.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
"I am the sunny
girl who brought you

00:31:21.079 --> 00:31:24.315 align:start size:97% position:3%
"this the most to make
your own sunshine stories."

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:25.717
And I said, "You know what?

00:31:25.717 --> 00:31:26.951
"I'm both.

00:31:26.951 --> 00:31:28.486
"Why can't I be both?"

00:31:28.486 --> 00:31:29.787
Because I am both.

00:31:29.787 --> 00:31:33.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
I am still the
glass is half full.

00:31:33.291 --> 00:31:35.693
I'm still the mostly sunny girl.

00:31:35.693 --> 00:31:38.630
I'm also an advocate
who wants to shine

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:41.633
sunlight as a disinfectant.

00:31:41.633 --> 00:31:43.167
So, you know,

00:31:43.167 --> 00:31:47.472
I think the advocacy can also
be making your own sunshine.

00:31:47.472 --> 00:31:50.174 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, you know, trying to
balance both of those things

00:31:50.174 --> 00:31:52.176
is I can be both
at the same time,

00:31:52.176 --> 00:31:53.711
but I'm not gonna lie.

00:31:53.711 --> 00:31:55.980
Sometimes the good news
stories are a tough sell.

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:58.283
- Excellent use of the
metaphor with sunshine

00:31:58.283 --> 00:32:01.786
as the best disinfectant that
fully works with the book.

00:32:01.786 --> 00:32:03.955
Give me like two
stories that you,

00:32:03.955 --> 00:32:06.157 align:start size:88% position:13%
that really
particularly moved you.

00:32:06.157 --> 00:32:08.192 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can pick one or two,
but if you got some

00:32:08.192 --> 00:32:10.161
that are right off
the top of your head?

00:32:10.161 --> 00:32:11.629
- Well, it's funny,

00:32:11.629 --> 00:32:14.465 align:start position:0%
because everyone tells me a
different story that they love.

00:32:14.465 --> 00:32:16.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's what I love
about it is that, you know,

00:32:16.768 --> 00:32:19.971 align:start size:88% position:13%
someone will say, "Well,
I loved, you know,

00:32:19.971 --> 00:32:21.706
"the Carrie Blazey story."

00:32:21.706 --> 00:32:22.573
So I'll tell you that one.

00:32:22.573 --> 00:32:25.376
That was the first one,

00:32:25.376 --> 00:32:27.745 align:start position:0%
one of the first interviews
that I did during the pandemic,

00:32:27.745 --> 00:32:31.115
and Carrie and I became very
close friends, afterwards.

00:32:31.115 --> 00:32:32.617
Simple story.

00:32:32.617 --> 00:32:36.988 align:start size:94% position:6%
Her daughter was diagnosed
with type one diabetes,

00:32:36.988 --> 00:32:38.523
forgive me type two diabetes,

00:32:38.523 --> 00:32:40.525
what's the more serious one?

00:32:40.525 --> 00:32:41.459
Forgive me.

00:32:43.094 --> 00:32:44.429
I don't wanna screw this up,

00:32:44.429 --> 00:32:45.763
so I'm gonna get
my book right now.

00:32:45.763 --> 00:32:46.998
- This is impressive.

00:32:46.998 --> 00:32:49.000
An author actually
going to the source.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:50.435
I like it.

00:32:50.435 --> 00:32:51.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well it's interesting --
- Wait, I've got,

00:32:51.402 --> 00:32:52.704
let's see who can find it first?

00:32:52.704 --> 00:32:55.006
- I don't wanna screw it
up because there's this

00:32:55.006 --> 00:32:59.210
really, okay, there's
two types of diabetes,

00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:01.980
and the one that she has
is the really serious one,

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:04.048 align:start size:81% position:19%
type one.
- Type one, okay.

00:33:04.048 --> 00:33:05.450
And that's why it's important,

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:08.886 align:start position:0%
because Carrie is a big
advocate for type one diabetes,

00:33:08.886 --> 00:33:12.190
because that's, you know,
not to say that type two

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:15.059
is not a serious
illness as well,

00:33:15.059 --> 00:33:18.629
but type one is when
you're really really

00:33:18.629 --> 00:33:20.798 align:start size:84% position:16%
constantly concerned
about your child,

00:33:20.798 --> 00:33:23.201
every day they take her,
you know her measurements

00:33:23.201 --> 00:33:24.702
or glucose levels.

00:33:24.702 --> 00:33:26.804
She's got a special, you know,

00:33:26.804 --> 00:33:31.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
a special portal on her body
that has to be, you know,

00:33:31.209 --> 00:33:34.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
her insulin has to be just
right, the right level.

00:33:34.812 --> 00:33:39.817
So having said that, Carrie
put a sign on her door saying

00:33:40.985 --> 00:33:42.687
that she had a person
who was compromised

00:33:42.687 --> 00:33:43.921
living in her house.

00:33:43.921 --> 00:33:45.089 align:start size:81% position:19%
And as a person
who lives with MS,

00:33:45.089 --> 00:33:46.924 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can totally
identify with that.

00:33:46.924 --> 00:33:48.126
At the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:33:48.126 --> 00:33:49.727
we didn't know how bad it was.

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:52.430
She didn't want the virus
anywhere near her daughter,

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:53.664
her compromised daughter,

00:33:53.664 --> 00:33:55.433
so she put a sign on
the door that said

00:33:55.433 --> 00:33:58.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
"If you have any deliveries,
leave the package here.

00:33:58.102 --> 00:33:59.637
"Don't ring my doorbell.

00:33:59.637 --> 00:34:01.806
"I'm not opening up the
door, just leave it here,

00:34:01.806 --> 00:34:04.709
"and I will sanitize the
package and bring it in."

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:09.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
So a wonderful FedEx driver
sees the sign and says,

00:34:10.882 --> 00:34:12.650
"Oh", goes back to
his FedEx truck,

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:14.585 align:start size:84% position:16%
they see this, by
the way, on a camera,

00:34:14.585 --> 00:34:16.187
he doesn't know that
he's being filmed.

00:34:16.187 --> 00:34:19.457
They have one of those nest
cameras on their doorstep.

00:34:19.457 --> 00:34:21.159
He goes back to the FedEx truck,

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:24.662
grabs some wipes and
wipes the package.

00:34:24.662 --> 00:34:27.031 align:start size:84% position:16%
He smudges the print
on the package,

00:34:27.031 --> 00:34:31.436
and thinks to himself, "Oh
I don't want them to wonder

00:34:31.436 --> 00:34:33.571
"why the package is smudged."

00:34:33.571 --> 00:34:36.707
So he writes a note saying,
"I saw your note on the door.

00:34:36.707 --> 00:34:39.644 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I sanitized the package,
have a great day."

00:34:39.644 --> 00:34:42.013
He didn't expect
anything from that.

00:34:42.013 --> 00:34:45.450
She saw the video of
this kind FedEx driver,

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:47.085
and thought to herself, "Wow.

00:34:47.085 --> 00:34:49.187
"He went the extra mile,

00:34:49.187 --> 00:34:52.223
"took the extra step to
do this for my family."

00:34:52.223 --> 00:34:54.092
And her husband was
the one who said

00:34:54.092 --> 00:34:56.127
"You should put that
out on social media.

00:34:56.127 --> 00:34:57.562
"Maybe he'll get a raise

00:34:57.562 --> 00:35:00.398 align:start size:97% position:3%
"or FedEx will do something
nice for this driver."

00:35:00.398 --> 00:35:02.133
And she put it on social media.

00:35:02.133 --> 00:35:03.668
It went viral.

00:35:03.668 --> 00:35:06.671
FedEx certainly,
you know, saw that,

00:35:06.671 --> 00:35:09.774
Justin Bradshaw, by the
way, is the FedEx driver.

00:35:09.774 --> 00:35:13.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
And as it turns out,
Justin has a little girl

00:35:13.111 --> 00:35:15.012
that when she was
born was a preemie,

00:35:15.012 --> 00:35:16.247
and they had to
take care of her,

00:35:16.247 --> 00:35:17.515
and they were worried about her.

00:35:17.515 --> 00:35:20.785
So his backstory sort of
reflects upon, you know,

00:35:20.785 --> 00:35:23.287
why he was doing
what he was doing.

00:35:23.287 --> 00:35:26.724
So that's the story of Carrie
Blazey and her daughter, Emma,

00:35:26.724 --> 00:35:29.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the wonderful
FedEx driver, Justin,

00:35:29.427 --> 00:35:33.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
who did a kind thing that
deserves all of our attention,

00:35:33.064 --> 00:35:35.500
and that's why I started
the book with that chapter.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:37.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and it's interesting
because it's like,

00:35:37.602 --> 00:35:39.904
a sort of at face
value what he did

00:35:39.904 --> 00:35:42.006
wasn't that extraordinary,

00:35:42.006 --> 00:35:43.774
except I think what
people have realized

00:35:43.774 --> 00:35:47.278
in this past year is that
it is these little things

00:35:47.278 --> 00:35:49.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are becoming
sort of extraordinary

00:35:49.113 --> 00:35:50.982
as we socially distance,

00:35:50.982 --> 00:35:52.717
and we don't hug people anymore,

00:35:52.717 --> 00:35:55.786
and you wear a mask, and
sunglasses, and a hat,

00:35:55.786 --> 00:35:57.388
and then people don't
even see your face,

00:35:57.388 --> 00:36:00.758
but these little things, was
that like a theme for you,

00:36:00.758 --> 00:36:03.528
that little things have
somehow become bigger?

00:36:03.528 --> 00:36:05.663 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, so there are definitely
the little things in there,

00:36:05.663 --> 00:36:08.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
like buying someone's coffee
in line and then doing it

00:36:08.299 --> 00:36:10.968
for the next person and
someone buying a coffee

00:36:10.968 --> 00:36:12.803 align:start size:81% position:19%
for you can change
someone's day.

00:36:12.803 --> 00:36:15.106
It's that simple.

00:36:15.106 --> 00:36:17.508 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I also talked
about bigger movements,

00:36:17.508 --> 00:36:20.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
for instance my
husband's friend Ray,

00:36:20.378 --> 00:36:22.180
well he was my friend too.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:27.185 align:start position:0%
Ray Pfeifer, FDNY, he was there
after the Twin Towers fell.

00:36:28.219 --> 00:36:29.420
- Yeah, this is the one

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:30.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I wanted to
ask you about, yeah.

00:36:30.521 --> 00:36:32.490
- Digging for his friends

00:36:32.490 --> 00:36:37.495 align:start size:97% position:3%
for months in toxic, a
toxic stew that we now know

00:36:39.564 --> 00:36:43.301
was so bad that it caused
9/11-related illnesses.

00:36:43.301 --> 00:36:45.903
Ray got very sick with cancer,

00:36:45.903 --> 00:36:47.772
and he was just
one of those guys

00:36:47.772 --> 00:36:50.141
that would walk into a room
and you just would gravitate,

00:36:50.141 --> 00:36:51.576
we called him Ray of light.

00:36:51.576 --> 00:36:53.311 align:start position:0%
We called him the mayor because
he would walk into a room

00:36:53.311 --> 00:36:54.912 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you would
gravitate towards Ray,

00:36:54.912 --> 00:36:56.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
he would do anything
for you, you know

00:36:56.981 --> 00:36:59.317
shirt off his back kind of guy.

00:36:59.317 --> 00:37:03.087
So he got sick with
9/11-related cancer,

00:37:03.087 --> 00:37:06.057
because he dug for
months trying to find

00:37:06.057 --> 00:37:07.758 align:start size:88% position:13%
his fellow firefighters
that you know,

00:37:07.758 --> 00:37:10.728
their families wanted to
at least find some remains.

00:37:12.196 --> 00:37:16.601
So he spent the rest of his
days after he was diagnosed

00:37:16.601 --> 00:37:19.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
making sure that
other first responders

00:37:19.537 --> 00:37:21.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
would never have to
worry about healthcare

00:37:21.939 --> 00:37:25.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
if they were diagnosed like
him with 9/11-related illness.

00:37:25.843 --> 00:37:30.248
So Ray screamed as
loud as he could,

00:37:30.248 --> 00:37:31.549
he went to Capitol Hill,

00:37:31.549 --> 00:37:33.551
he banged on Congress doors,

00:37:33.551 --> 00:37:38.155
Chuck Schumer, you know, like,
"Listen, I'm dying of cancer.

00:37:38.155 --> 00:37:42.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
"I wanna make sure my brothers
and sisters have healthcare

00:37:42.593 --> 00:37:44.996
"so that their families
don't have to worry.",

00:37:44.996 --> 00:37:48.299
because it's, you know,
their bills are high.

00:37:48.299 --> 00:37:52.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, you know, he's
no longer with us,

00:37:52.069 --> 00:37:57.074
but I call, he was making
sunshine for his friends

00:37:57.074 --> 00:37:58.476
and his fellow firefighters,

00:37:58.476 --> 00:38:01.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
and his fellow first
responders like my husband.

00:38:01.245 --> 00:38:03.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know that if my
husband ever got sick,

00:38:03.614 --> 00:38:05.249
Ray would take care of him,

00:38:05.249 --> 00:38:06.851
and he would take
care of our family.

00:38:06.851 --> 00:38:08.953 align:start size:88% position:13%
So that was an important
story for me to tell,

00:38:08.953 --> 00:38:10.221
and you know what?

00:38:10.221 --> 00:38:12.056
When I think about
my fight right now

00:38:12.056 --> 00:38:15.426
with the Governor, I
think of Ray Pfeifer,

00:38:15.426 --> 00:38:17.261 align:start size:84% position:16%
because he did
everything he could

00:38:17.261 --> 00:38:20.831
to raise awareness for good.

00:38:20.831 --> 00:38:22.266
- You know, it's interesting,

00:38:22.266 --> 00:38:23.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
because when I was reading
the story about Ray,

00:38:23.567 --> 00:38:25.469
I was in New York
also during 9/11,

00:38:25.469 --> 00:38:27.004
as I said, I'm from Long Island,

00:38:27.004 --> 00:38:29.740
but I lived in Manhattan on
the Upper East during 9/11.

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:32.510
And I remember about
two weeks after 9/11,

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:33.878
when people finally started

00:38:33.878 --> 00:38:35.479
even just leaving
their houses again,

00:38:35.479 --> 00:38:37.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause we were all
glued to the television,

00:38:37.081 --> 00:38:40.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
and also the smell out
there and everything else.

00:38:40.017 --> 00:38:42.286
I remember I was on
a basketball court.

00:38:42.286 --> 00:38:43.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went to play basketball
by Gracie Mansion.

00:38:43.954 --> 00:38:45.389
I was like, I've gotta
get out of the house.

00:38:45.389 --> 00:38:47.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just like, I can't
stay in my house anymore.

00:38:47.458 --> 00:38:48.959
And I was playing basketball

00:38:48.959 --> 00:38:51.162 align:start size:97% position:3%
with just a couple of random
guys and somebody elbowed

00:38:51.162 --> 00:38:53.698
one guy and they got
into each other's face,

00:38:53.698 --> 00:38:55.333
and they were about
to hit each other,

00:38:55.333 --> 00:38:56.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
like you could, I've seen
this a million times,

00:38:56.867 --> 00:38:58.336
it was about to happen,

00:38:58.336 --> 00:39:01.405 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they both started crying
at the exact same time.

00:39:01.405 --> 00:39:02.940
Both started crying.

00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:04.975
Like I'm getting chills up my
spine as I'm telling you this.

00:39:04.975 --> 00:39:07.311
And so I was thinking about
that when I was reading this,

00:39:07.311 --> 00:39:10.147 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it's like
those little moments

00:39:10.147 --> 00:39:13.484
of humanity, that is
the important stuff,

00:39:13.484 --> 00:39:16.687 align:start position:0%
and it's sort of like, we could
use a little more of that.

00:39:16.687 --> 00:39:19.557
I hate to say it, but
that sort of post-9/11

00:39:19.557 --> 00:39:21.559
like yeah, we all kind
of are in this together,

00:39:21.559 --> 00:39:22.927
whether you like it or not.

00:39:24.128 --> 00:39:25.796
- I agree, I hope
we can get there.

00:39:25.796 --> 00:39:28.165
I don't know that
we can right now,

00:39:28.165 --> 00:39:30.301
I feel like people are angry,

00:39:30.301 --> 00:39:32.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're rightfully
angry, Dave.

00:39:32.103 --> 00:39:33.938
I mean, we've been
trapped in our homes,

00:39:33.938 --> 00:39:35.906
it depends on where
you live, obviously,

00:39:35.906 --> 00:39:37.341
but here in New York,

00:39:37.341 --> 00:39:39.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean people still can't
see their loved ones

00:39:39.076 --> 00:39:40.711
in nursing homes,

00:39:40.711 --> 00:39:43.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
people have lost
their businesses and
their livelihoods.

00:39:43.881 --> 00:39:46.384
I mean, this is tough.

00:39:46.384 --> 00:39:50.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
I hope we can get to a point
where we can, you know,

00:39:50.154 --> 00:39:51.689
smile at each other again.

00:39:51.689 --> 00:39:54.058
And, but having said that,

00:39:54.058 --> 00:39:56.527 align:start size:84% position:16%
I do see those
pieces of humanity.

00:39:56.527 --> 00:39:59.296
You know, what we went
through with our family,

00:39:59.296 --> 00:40:03.067 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, from the
firefighter down the street

00:40:03.067 --> 00:40:04.969
that brought us
a meal, you know,

00:40:04.969 --> 00:40:07.972
just dropped it on our doorstep,

00:40:07.972 --> 00:40:10.841 align:start size:94% position:6%
to all of the prayer cards
and prayers that we got

00:40:10.841 --> 00:40:14.211 align:start size:97% position:3%
from other people, our
church, obviously, you know,

00:40:14.211 --> 00:40:16.180
opened their arms,

00:40:16.180 --> 00:40:19.183
there were parishes all
over New York that said

00:40:19.183 --> 00:40:21.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they were gonna
honor my husband's family.

00:40:21.919 --> 00:40:23.587
So it's there.

00:40:23.587 --> 00:40:25.055
And like I say in the book,

00:40:25.055 --> 00:40:28.492
sometimes you just have
to really look for it,

00:40:28.492 --> 00:40:29.760
because if you're
not open to it,

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:31.862
and you don't open
your heart to it,

00:40:31.862 --> 00:40:33.531
then it might not come to you,

00:40:33.531 --> 00:40:35.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
so I think that maybe that's
what we have to do is,

00:40:35.433 --> 00:40:37.001
we have to look for it.

